  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Tranquil weather takes over through Saturday\nthough a few very isolated sea-breeze showers cannot be ruled \nout this afternoon.\n\nShowers have dissipated for tonight and we should be dry through\nthe rest of the morning. However, some patchy fog could develop\ngiven the residual ground moisture and some clearing of clouds,\nparticularly in sheltered areas. While confidence is not high \nin fog development, went ahead and kept patchy fog in the forecast\ngiven the potential for lowered visibilities in spots. Any fog\nmixes out by 7-8 AM. \n\nSurface high pressure settles in today with drier air moving in. Some\nguidance hinting at a few rather isolated showers this\nafternoon, mainly focused along the sea and bay breeze. Did add\na mention of showers near the NJ and Delaware coast, but only\n15% for a brief period this afternoon. A large majority today\nwill stay dry however given the closed low/trough continuing to\nmove away. Some warming aloft along with some increase in \nsunshine Friday will result in warmer afternoon temperatures. \nLighter flow however will result in a sea/bay breeze developing \nby later in the afternoon. Depending on how far inland this gets\nby early Friday evening, some increase in the dew points should\noccur in its wake. Temperatures moderate some more to start the\nweekend with high temperatures on Saturday right near average \nalong with dry conditions. \n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...A frontal system on Sunday Night and Monday \nwill bring the next opportunity for widespread showers and \nthunderstorms.\n\nHigh pressure weakens late Saturday and is replaced by \nadvancing low pressure from the Ohio Valley area. This low \nstrengthens and moves west of our area later Sunday and into \nMonday. This system will bring increasing chances for showers\nand thunderstorms later Sunday, with a bulk of the precipitation\ncoming in Sunday Night into Monday.  The PWAT values off the \nforecast soundings during these times show more 2+ inch PWAT \nvalues for our region, so it's possible that excessive rainfall \nmay develop for parts of the region. Latest outlook from WPC \nshows a marginal risk of excessive rainfall. Moisture and \ninstability will also increase ahead of the front, so we likely \nwill have to monitor the severe weather threat for the Sunday \nand Monday afternoon periods as well. However, our area is not \nin any long term severe outlooks, so there is a chance that \nthere are more hydro concerns with this system.\n\nGuidance continues to show this as a slow moving system with a\nfrontal boundary either stalling over our area or slowly pushing\nto south and east. This could result in another stretch of\nunsettled weather through next week. The current forecast does\nhave showers/thunderstorm chances each day next week, mainly in\nthe afternoon and evening. Important to note though that each\nday other than maybe Monday will have periods of tranquil\nweather but it does turn to look more active after Sunday.",
